# Hack The Box - Soccer
> OS: Linux  
> Attack Path: Default Credentials → Tiny File Manager RCE → WebSocket SQL Injection → Credential Dumping → doas/dstat Privilege Escalation
> Youtube Help: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P38IKp9S1ts
---

# Summary

The **Soccer** machine starts with the discovery of a vulnerable **Tiny File Manager** instance exposed on the web server. Using default credentials, it is possible to gain administrative access and upload a malicious PHP file to obtain a reverse shell as **www-data**.

During local enumeration, a virtual host named **soc-player.soccer.htb** is discovered. The application behind this virtual host uses a **WebSocket** backend that is vulnerable to **Blind SQL Injection**. Exploiting this SQLi allows extraction of credentials for the **player** user.

Finally, the machine can be fully compromised because the **player** account is allowed to run **dstat** as root through **doas**. By abusing the plugin functionality of dstat, arbitrary Python code is executed as root, resulting in a root shell.

